<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Jaunpur Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1394 CE – 1479 CE · capital Jaunpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Sharqi sultanate of the eastern Ganges plain, whose capital Jaunpur was a centre of learning and of a distinctive mosque architecture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1394 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughluq</a> sultan appointed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-sarwar">Malik Sarwar</a> governor of the eastern provinces with the title Malik al-Sharq, lord of the east. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-delhi-1398">sack of Delhi</a> in 1398 Malik Sarwar ruled independently from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a>, founding the Sharqi dynasty, which was continued by his adopted son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mubarak-shah-sharqi">Mubarak Shah</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-shah-sharqi">Ibrahim Shah Sharqi</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a> a refuge for scholars fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, earning it the name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atala-mosque">Atala Mosque</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jama-masjid-jaunpur">Jama Masjid of Jaunpur</a>, with their massive gateway screens, are the finest examples of the Sharqi style. The sultans held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Doab</a> and frequently challenged the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husain-shah-sharqi">Husain Shah Sharqi's</a> repeated attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> failed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahlul-lodi">Bahlul Lodi</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a> in 1479. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Husain Shah</a> took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and Jaunpur became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> sultanate and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jaunpur-sultanate.jpg" alt="Jaunpur Sultanate" width="320" height="158"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaunpur_Sultante,_Shams_al-Din_Ibrahim_Shah,_dated_1438.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1394 CE – 1479 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jaunpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Malik Sarwar, Ibrahim Shah Sharqi, Husain Shah Sharqi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Hindustani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sharqi dynasty, Sharqi Sultanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1436&amp;focus=jaunpur-sultanate" aria-label="Jaunpur Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6867 1231 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jaunpur Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7187 1487L7018 1385M7187 1487L7275 1464"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7275 1464h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7187 1487h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7187 1487h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jaunpur. <a href="/atlas?year=1436&amp;focus=jaunpur-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
